            <ArticleTitle>On the horizon: research priorities in burns for the next decade.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>917-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.suc.2014.05.012</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0039-6109(14)00075-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This review demonstrates that many advances have been made in burn care that have made dramatic differences in mortality, clinical outcomes, and quality of life in burn survivors; however, much work remains. In reality, the current standard of care is insufficient and we cannot be satisfied with the status quo. We must strive for the following goals: no deaths due to burn, no scarring, and no pain. These particular goals have only begun to be confronted.</AbstractText>
